nd function Long-term scar management and follow-up care support CO2 lasers are commonly used for skin resurfacing, scar revision, burn scar management, and wrinkle reduction through controlled removal of damaged skin layers. Fractional laser systems deliver targeted microscopic treatment zones to stimulate collagen production and skin regeneration with reduced recovery time. IPL systems use broad-spectrum light energy for pigmentation correction, vascular lesion treatment, acne management, and long-term hair reduction. These technologies are widely used in cosmetic dermatology, plastic surgery, burn rehabilitation, and aesthetic medicine. Common Uses Scar revision and burn scar management Skin resurfacing and wrinkle reduction procedures Pigmentation correction and skin tone improvement Hair reduction and aesthetic skin treatments Acne scars, vascular lesions, and skin rejuvenation procedures Key Components CO2 laser systems for resurfacing and scar treatment Fractional laser devices for collagen stimulation and skin repair IPL systems for pigmentation and hair reduction treatments Cooling and skin protection systems for patient safety Precision delivery handpieces and treatment control interfaces Risks & Challenges Temporary redness, swelling, or skin sensitivity after treatment Risk of burns, pigmentation changes, or scarring if improperly used Need for protective eyewear and strict laser safety protocols Multiple sessions may be required for optimal outcomes Post-treatment sun sensitivity and skin care requirements Treatment Applications Laser equipment supports aesthetic dermatology, scar management, burn rehabilitation, anti-aging treatment, reconstructive cosmetic procedures, pigmentation correction, and minimally invasive skin rejuvenation therapies. These technologies help improve skin appearance, texture, elasticity, and overall patient confidence. Postoperative Care Regular skin moisturization and hydration support Sun protection and avoidance of excessiv